# Marsad migration ledger — the reviewed source of truth for which migrations exist.
#
# One line per migration: the filename stem (<version>_<name>, i.e. the .sql basename without
# the extension), sorted. CI (`scripts/check-migration-ledger.mjs`, wired into .github/workflows/ci.yml)
# FAILS the build if `supabase/migrations/*.sql` and this file diverge — so a migration can never be
# added, removed, or renamed without a reviewed change to this ledger.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS — the recurring MCP-apply drift trap:
#   Applying a migration to live via the Supabase MCP `apply_migration` tool auto-generates the
#   ledger `version` from the wall-clock at apply time (NOT from your filename), and stores the
#   filename as `name`. So the committed `20260716100000_fleet_productivity_guard.sql` landed on
#   live as version `20260716060948`. Repo and live schema_migrations then hold the SAME migrations
#   under DIFFERENT version strings — silent drift a from-scratch replay/rebuild would get wrong.
#   PR #22 fixed it once; it recurred within two days (reconciled 2026-07-16, see
#   supabase/reconcile/20260716_reconcile_migration_ledger.sql).
#
# THE DISCIPLINE (do this every time you touch migrations — it is what makes the guard bite):
#   1. Author the migration as a committed `supabase/migrations/<version>_<name>.sql`.
#   2. Apply it. If you used the MCP `apply_migration`, IMMEDIATELY check that the live
#      `supabase_migrations.schema_migrations` row's `version` == your filename's version. If the MCP
#      auto-generated a different version, re-stamp it (metadata-only UPDATE) so live == filename.
#   3. Regenerate this ledger + confirm CI passes:  `node scripts/check-migration-ledger.mjs --write`
#      Review the diff — a surprise line means drift you must reconcile against LIVE, not paper over.
#
# NOTE (2026-07-16): the live DB additionally carries version `20260716121312`
# (fix_heartbeat_sentinel_session_source_aware), applied out-of-band by a concurrent session while
# this ledger was reconciled. It is NOT in this ledger because its .sql is not committed here — its
# owning change must add both the `20260716121312_*.sql` file AND its ledger line (CI will require it).

